Looking at this problem, I need to determine whether the condition (all roots in 𝔻, one root is an n-th root of z) forces all roots to be n-th roots of z.

**Answer: Yes.**

**Proof.**

Define the holomorphic function
$$g(w) = P(w, w^n) = w^n + f_1(w^n)w^{n-1} + \cdots + f_n(w^n), \quad w \in \mathbb{D}.$$

**Step 1: $g \equiv 0$ on $\mathbb{D}$.**

The hypothesis says: for every $z \in \mathbb{D}$, some root $w$ of $P(\cdot, z)$ satisfies $w^n = z$. Since all roots lie in $\mathbb{D}$, this $w \in \mathbb{D}$, and $P(w, w^n) = 0$, i.e., $g(w) = 0$. Therefore
$$\{w^n : g(w) = 0,\; w \in \mathbb{D}\} = \mathbb{D}.$$

If $g \not\equiv 0$, then the zeros of $g$ form a discrete subset of $\mathbb{D}$, which is at most countable (since $\mathbb{D}$ is second-countable). The image of a countable set under $w \mapsto w^n$ is at most countable, contradicting the image being all of $\mathbb{D}$ (which is uncountable). Hence $g \equiv 0$.

**Step 2: The zero set $S$ contains the surface $T$.**

Let $S = \{(z,w) \in \mathbb{D}\times\mathbb{D} : P(w,z) = 0\}$ and $T = \{(z,w) \in \mathbb{D}\times\mathbb{D} : w^n = z\}$. The identity $g \equiv 0$ means $P(w, w^n) = 0$ for all $w \in \mathbb{D}$, i.e., $T \subseteq S$.

**Step 3: $S = T$, so all roots are $n$-th roots of $z$.**

For any $z \neq 0$ in $\mathbb{D}$, the set $T_z = \{w : w^n = z\}$ consists of $n$ distinct points, all lying in $\mathbb{D}$ (since $|w| = |z|^{1/n} < 1$). Since $T \subseteq S$, all $n$ points of $T_z$ lie in $S_z = \{$roots of $P(\cdot,z)\}$. But $P(\cdot, z)$ is a monic polynomial of degree $n$, so $S_z$ has at most $n$ elements. Therefore $S_z = T_z$ for every $z \neq 0$.

Consequently, for each $z \neq 0$, the polynomial $P(\cdot, z)$ has exactly the $n$ distinct $n$-th roots of $z$ as its roots. Being monic of degree $n$:
$$P(w, z) = w^n - z \quad \text{for all } z \neq 0.$$

By holomorphicity of the coefficients $f_k(z)$, this identity extends to $z = 0$ as well, giving $P(w, z) = w^n - z$ on all of $\mathbb{D}$. At $z = 0$, the only $n$-th root is $0$ (with multiplicity $n$), and indeed $P(w, 0) = w^n$.

Therefore, for every $z \in \mathbb{D}$, all roots of $P(\cdot, z)$ are $n$-th roots of $z$. $\blacksquare$

### PROOF COMPLETE
